<% content_for :title, "Vitral" %>

<%= render layout: "shared/shell" do %>
  <header class="gallery-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h1>Vitral</h1>
    <p>
      Janela publishes the hooks and a theme supplies the taste (ADR 036).
      <code>janela.css</code> is the grid, the pane's own markup and enough style to be legible;
      <code>vitral.css</code> is one theme, and this whole site wears it. Everything below is drawn
      live against this demo's own data, with the classes and properties that produced it named beside
      it. <%= link_to "The full contract", doc_path("theming") %> lists every one.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>Two stylesheets</h2>
    <p>Link the first always, the second only if you want this look.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="t-e">&lt;%=</span> <span class="t-m">stylesheet_link_tag</span> <span class="t-str">&quot;janela&quot;</span> <span class="t-e">%&gt;</span>
<span class="t-e">&lt;%=</span> <span class="t-m">stylesheet_link_tag</span> <span class="t-str">&quot;vitral&quot;</span> <span class="t-e">%&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="t-k">body</span> <span class="t-l">class=</span><span class="t-str">&quot;vitral&quot;</span>&gt;</code></pre>
    <p class="theming-note">
      Nothing is repainted until that class is present, so linking the stylesheet can never be the thing
      that broke a page. A theme of your own is the same one line: <code>Janela.theme = "midnight"</code>
      resolves <code>midnight.css</code> from your own asset paths, and vitral is only the one the gem ships.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>The grid</h2>
    <p>
      <code>janela-cols-1</code> to <code>12</code>, <code>janela-gap-0</code> to <code>8</code>, and
      <code>janela-span-1</code> to <code>12</code>. A frame saved as a record gets them from its own stored
      integers, so nothing an analyst types reaches CSS as a length: the number selects a rule that is
      already written (ADR 016). On a page you compose yourself, you write the same class names.
    </p>

    <pre><code><span class="t-e">&lt;%=</span> <span class="t-m">janela_frame</span> <span class="t-k">do</span> <span class="t-e">%&gt;</span>
  &lt;<span class="t-k">div</span> <span class="t-l">class=</span><span class="t-str">&quot;janela-frame janela-cols-3 janela-gap-4&quot;</span>&gt;
    <span class="t-e">&lt;%=</span> <span class="t-m">janela_pane</span> <span class="t-c">Order</span>, <span class="t-s">:revenue</span> <span class="t-e">%&gt;</span>
    <span class="t-e">...</span>
  &lt;/<span class="t-k">div</span>&gt;
<span class="t-e">&lt;%</span> <span class="t-k">end</span> <span class="t-e">%&gt;</span></code></pre>

    <%= janela_frame do %>
      <div class="janela-frame janela-cols-3 janela-gap-4">
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue %>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :orders %>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :average_order %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <p class="theming-note">three columns, gap 4</p>

    <%= janela_frame do %>
      <div class="janela-frame janela-cols-3 janela-gap-4">
        <div class="janela-span-2"><%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, as: :bar %></div>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :region %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
    <p class="theming-note">
      the same grid, with the first pane wrapped in <code>janela-span-2</code>. A pane is the grid item, and
      <code>janela_pane</code> takes no class of its own, and will not, so a wrapper is how a hand composed
      page spans one. A stored frame does it from the integer on the pane row instead. Arranging panes
      beyond the shipped grid is the host's.
    </p>
    <p class="theming-note">Below <code>40rem</code> it collapses to one column, from the stylesheet rather
      than from the data: a dashboard nobody can read on a phone is not a choice worth offering.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>The pane</h2>
    <p>
      What <code>janela_pane</code> puts in your own page, whatever the renderer:
      <code>janela-pane</code> on the table, the value's <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> or the chart's
      <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code>, plus <code>janela-value</code>, <code>janela-value-label</code>
      and <code>janela-value-number</code> on a single value, <code>janela-chart</code> on the
      canvas inside its figure and <code>janela-chart-title</code> on its figcaption, and
      <code>janela-empty</code> or <code>janela-error</code> when there is nothing to draw. A
      table pane also renders a <code>&lt;caption&gt;</code> and a chart pane a
      <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code>, each its own accessible name (ADR 042).
    </p>

    <%= janela_frame do %>
      <div class="janela-frame janela-cols-2 janela-gap-4">
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue %>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>Headings you already wrote</h2>
    <p>
      Put <code>janela-own-headings</code> on any ancestor and a caption and a single value's label stop
      being drawn, without leaving the accessibility tree. It hides rather than removes on purpose: a table
      with no caption has no accessible name, so <code>display: none</code> would land a screen reader on a
      grid of numbers with nothing to say what they measure (#26).
    </p>

    <div class="theming-pair">
      <div id="plain-example">
        <p class="theming-label">Without it</p>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, id: "plain-caption" %>
        <p class="theming-note">Janela writes the caption: <strong>Revenue by Status</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <div id="own-headings-example" class="janela-own-headings">
        <p class="theming-label">With <code>janela-own-headings</code></p>
        <h3 class="theming-own-heading">How the quarter split</h3>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, id: "quiet-caption" %>
        <p class="theming-note">
          Janela's caption is gone from the page and still in the accessibility tree. The heading above the
          table is this page's own <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>, saying something Janela would never have written.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="theming-note">
      Both tables still announce "Revenue by Status" to a screen reader, which is the whole point: the second
      one has a visible heading of its own and an accessible name underneath it, rather than one heading
      doing both jobs badly.
    </p>

    <p>
      A chart pane's title used to be only an <code>aria-label</code>, announced and never seen, which
      <code>janela-own-headings</code> had nothing to hide. Since ADR 042 it is a real
      <code>&lt;figcaption&gt;</code>, so the same rule now hides it the same way.
    </p>

    <div class="theming-pair">
      <div id="plain-chart-example">
        <p class="theming-label">Without it</p>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, as: :bar, id: "plain-chart-caption" %>
        <p class="theming-note">Janela writes the figcaption: <strong>Revenue by Status</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <div id="own-headings-chart-example" class="janela-own-headings">
        <p class="theming-label">With <code>janela-own-headings</code></p>
        <h3 class="theming-own-heading">How the quarter split</h3>
        <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, as: :bar, id: "quiet-chart-caption" %>
        <p class="theming-note">
          Janela's figcaption is gone from the page and still in the accessibility tree; the canvas's
          <code>aria-labelledby</code> points at it either way, hidden or not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="theming-note">Both charts still announce "Revenue by Status" to a screen reader, same as the tables above.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>The chart palette</h2>
    <p>
      A bar, a doughnut slice or a pie slice is drawn in the colour for its position, first to eighth, and
      in one neutral for everything after the eighth. The colours are custom properties, so a theme sets
      them: <code>--janela-series-1</code> to <code>-8</code> and <code>--janela-series-other</code>. Slot 1
      follows <code>--janela-accent</code>. These are vitral's, drawn from the live styles.
    </p>
    <div id="palette">
      <%= render "pages/palette_swatches" %>
      <div class="theming-pair">
        <div class="vitral-pane demo-tile">
          <%= janela_frame do %>
            <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :customer, limit: 8, as: :doughnut, id: "palette-ring" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <div class="vitral-pane demo-tile">
          <%= janela_frame do %>
            <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :placed_on, granularity: :month, as: :bar, id: "palette-bars" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="theming-note">
      A ring suits a handful of values. The bar chart is revenue by month, thirteen bars, so the last five
      share the neutral: a ninth bar in the first bar's colour would be two categories drawn the same. The order is
      what keeps neighbours apart, checked together for colour vision deficiency, so a reorder has to be
      checked again (ADR 046).
    </p>
    <p>
      The same nine names under Janela's own neutral set, which is what a host gets without vitral. A theme
      or a host replaces the set by setting the properties and nothing else:
    </p>
    <pre><code><span class="t-k">:root</span> {
  <span class="t-l">--janela-series-2</span>: <span class="t-s">#c93c54</span>;
  <span class="t-l">--janela-series-3</span>: <span class="t-s">#7d5bd1</span>;
  <span class="t-c">/* ... through -8, and --janela-series-other */</span>
}</code></pre>
    <div id="palette-rethemed" class="theming-palette-neutral">
      <%= render "pages/palette_swatches" %>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>Retheming without forking</h2>
    <p>
      A few custom properties carry <code>janela.css</code>: <code>--janela-space</code>, the base unit of
      the whole spacing scale, <code>--janela-line</code> and <code>--janela-accent</code>, with the chart
      palette above. Set them and everything that depends on them moves.
    </p>
    <pre><code><span class="t-k">:root</span> {
  <span class="t-l">--janela-space</span>: <span class="t-s">0.3rem</span>;
  <span class="t-l">--janela-accent</span>: <span class="t-s">#7c3aed</span>;
}</code></pre>

    <div class="theming-retheme">
      <%= janela_frame do %>
        <div class="janela-frame janela-cols-2 janela-gap-4">
          <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :region, id: "rethemed-region" %>
          <%= janela_pane Order, :revenue, by: :status, as: :bar, id: "rethemed-status-chart" %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <p class="theming-note">
      the same panes, under a block that sets those two properties and nothing else; the bar chart's
      colour moves with <code>--janela-accent</code> too, rather than staying Chart.js's own blue (#62)
    </p>

    <p>
      Vitral has its own, and the same rule applies: retheme from the properties rather than by forking the
      stylesheet. The light is <code>--vitral-light-cobalt</code>, <code>-teal</code>, <code>-amber</code>,
      <code>-rose</code> and <code>-violet</code>; the glass is <code>--vitral-pane-1</code> through
      <code>-5</code>; the leading is <code>--vitral-came</code>. Its own public classes are
      <code>vitral-pane</code>, <code>vitral-panes</code>, <code>vitral-button</code>,
      <code>vitral-button-primary</code> and <code>vitral-lattice</code>, so the page around a dashboard can
      be made of the same window. Every card on this site is one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="theming-section">
    <h2>What is not yours to target</h2>
    <p>
      <code>janela.css</code> also styles Janela's own pages, the frame index and the editing forms. Those
      names are the engine's own chrome rather than an interface, and may change in any release
      (ADR 036). They are plain class names the engine's own pages use, not scoped to
      <code>janela-page</code>, so they affect your pages only if your markup uses the same names. Avoid them.
      <%= link_to "docs/theming.md", doc_path("theming") %> says which names are the contract and which are not.
    </p>
  </section>
<% end %>
